prompt.text:
No Terraform module in the catalog fits Port infra request ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}. The platform team delegated it to you: write a NEW reusable module AND the usage file for this request in this repository (port-demo/portcon-terraform-workshop), and open ONE pull request against main that is clearly marked as needing platform review.

## Request
- Infra request id: ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}
- Request: {{ .outputs.trigger.request_text }}
- Requested by: {{ .workflowRun.trigger.by.email }}
- Service: {{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}
- Environment: {{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}
- Data sensitivity: {{ .outputs.trigger.data_sensitivity }}
- Notes: {{ .outputs.trigger.notes // "none" }}
- Delegated by: {{ .outputs.platform_decision.responses[0].submitterInfo.submittedBy }} - reason: {{ .outputs.platform_decision.responses[0].inputs.reason }}
- Port Infra Request link: https://app.port.io/infra_requestEntity?identifier=ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}

## What to do
1. Study the repo first: list `modules/` and read at least two existing modules end to end (main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf, versions/providers if present, README.md), plus `envs/staging/example-s3.tf.example`, `.github/CODEOWNERS`, `.github/pull_request_template.md` and any CI workflow under `.github/workflows/`. Match their naming, file layout, variable style (descriptions, types, validation), tagging and README format exactly.
2. Create ONE new module under `modules/<new-module>/`, named like the existing modules (short, lowercase, describes the resource, e.g. `kafka-topic`). Make it reusable (inputs for the things that vary, safe defaults, the same `tags` convention as the other modules) and include a README in the same format as the others. Use only the providers the repo already uses; if the resource needs a provider the repo does not have, add it inside the module's own required_providers the same way other modules declare theirs.
3. Add EXACTLY ONE usage file: `envs/{{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}/{{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}-<new-module>-ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}.tf`, following the example file's structure: a single `module` block named `{{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}_<new module with underscores>`, `source = "../../modules/<new-module>"`, inputs that satisfy this request, and the six tags (service, team, environment, data_sensitivity, infra_request_id = ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}, managed_by = "port"). For the `team` tag use the platform team name from `.github/CODEOWNERS` (the part after @port-demo/).
4. If `.github/CODEOWNERS` has per-module lines, add one line for `modules/<new-module>/` owned by the platform team. Do not modify, rename or delete any other existing file.
5. The code must pass `terraform fmt -check` and `terraform validate` the same way the repo's CI runs them.
6. Branch: `infra/ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}`. One commit: `feat(module): <new-module> module + usage for {{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }} ({{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }})`.
7. PR title: `[new module][needs platform review] <new-module> for {{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }} ({{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }})`. PR body: fill in the repo's PR template `.github/pull_request_template.md` section by section (replace the HTML comments with real content; keep the headings and the footer line). Right after the Summary add exactly: `> 🛠️ NEW MODULE - needs platform team review. No catalog module fit this request; this PR was delegated to a coding agent by <delegated by> (reason: <reason>).` In the Guardrail Check section say the request failed guardrails only because no module existed, and list the rules below as-is. Open it as a normal, ready-for-review PR.

## Draft tech plan from the planner (context; it found no module)
{{ .outputs.draft_plan.tech_plan }}

## Guardrail details
{{ .outputs.check_guardrails.details }}

Implement approved Port infra request ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }} in this repository (port-demo/portcon-terraform-workshop) and open ONE pull request against main.

## Request
- Infra request id: ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}
- Request: {{ .outputs.trigger.request_text }}
- Requested by: {{ .workflowRun.trigger.by.email }}
- Service: {{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}
- Environment: {{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}
- Data sensitivity: {{ .outputs.trigger.data_sensitivity }}
- Module: {{ .outputs.draft_plan.module }} (source ../../modules/{{ .outputs.draft_plan.module }})
- Module owning team (use as the `team` tag): {{ .outputs.fetch_module.owning_team // "not looked up on this path: use the team that .github/CODEOWNERS lists for modules/<module>/ (the part after @port-demo/)" }}
- Approved inputs (JSON): {{ .outputs.draft_plan.inputs }}
- Guardrails: {{ .outputs.check_guardrails.result }}
- Approval: {{ if .outputs.exception_approval then "Approved as guardrail exception: " + (.outputs.exception_approval.responses[0].inputs.reason | tostring) + " (by " + (.outputs.exception_approval.responses[0].submitterInfo.submittedBy | tostring) + ")" elif .outputs.owner_approval then "Approved by " + (.outputs.owner_approval.responses[0].submitterInfo.submittedBy | tostring) + ": " + (.outputs.owner_approval.responses[0].inputs.reason | tostring) else "Auto-approved by rules" end }}
- Approval mode: {{ if .outputs.exception_approval then "exception" elif .outputs.owner_approval then "human" else "auto" end }}
- Port Infra Request link: https://app.port.io/infra_requestEntity?identifier=ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}

## What to change (strict)
1. Read `envs/staging/example-s3.tf.example` and the module's `modules/<module>/variables.tf` and `README.md` first.
2. Add EXACTLY ONE new file: `envs/{{ (.outputs.trigger.environment | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}/{{ (.outputs.trigger.service | if type == "object" then .identifier else . end) }}-{{ .outputs.draft_plan.module }}-ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}.tf`, following the example file's structure: a single `module` block named `<service>_<module with underscores>` (replace any other non-alphanumeric characters with underscores), `source = "../../modules/<module>"`, one line per approved input using exactly the approved values, and the six tags (service, team, environment, data_sensitivity, infra_request_id = ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}, managed_by = "port").
3. Do NOT modify, rename or delete any existing file. Never touch anything under `modules/`, `.github/`, providers.tf, lock files or READMEs. Do not add inputs the module does not define.
4. Branch: `infra/ir-{{ .workflowRun.identifier | gsub("wfr_"; "") | ascii_downcase }}`. One commit: `feat(infra): <module> for <service> (<environment>)`.
5. PR title: `[infra] <module> for <service> (<environment>)`. PR body: fill in the repo's PR template `.github/pull_request_template.md` section by section (replace the HTML comments with real content; keep the headings and the footer line). Open it as a normal, ready-for-review PR. If the approval mode above is `exception`, the PR body MUST contain, near the top (right after the Summary), a line exactly in the form `> ⚠️ Approved as guardrail exception: <reason> (by <approver>)` using the approval text above, and the Guardrail Check section must list the failed rules as-is. Use the approved inputs exactly as given even if they break a guardrail; do not make them safer.

## Approved tech plan (for the PR body)
{{ .outputs.draft_plan.tech_plan }}

## Guardrail details (for the PR body)
{{ .outputs.check_guardrails.details }}
